  "title": "Two Objects & Reflexives",
  "updatedDate": 0,
  "fileformat": 8,
  "description": "Practise dative recipients, accusative things, prepositions and reflexive forms.",
  "intro": "Some verbs commonly take two objects: the person receiving something is dative and the thing transferred is accusative. Separately, reflexive constructions can use either accusative or dative; prepositions have their own case rules.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Dative person + accusative thing",
      "sortorder": 1,
      "explanation": "The recipient is dative (wem?), while the item is accusative (was?). Both cases can appear in one sentence.",
      "rule": "jemandem (Dat.) + etwas (Akk.)",
      "entries": [
        {
          "header": "geben — to give",
          "kind": "conjugation",
          "explanation": "geben can take both a dative recipient (ihm) and an accusative item (das Buch).",
          "pattern": "geben + Dativ + Akkusativ",
          "examples": [
            {
              "german": "Ich gebe ihm das Buch.",
              "english": "I give him the book.",
              "focus": "ihm das Buch",
              "note": "dative recipient + accusative thing object",
              "key": "ihm"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du gibst ihm das Buch.",
              "english": "You (singular) give him the book.",
              "focus": "ihm das Buch",
              "note": "dative recipient + accusative thing object",
              "key": "ihm"
            },
            {
              "german": "Er gibt ihm das Buch.",
              "english": "He gives him the book.",
              "focus": "ihm das Buch",
              "note": "dative recipient + accusative thing object",
              "key": "ihm"
